rsonal moment is a cool change of  pace for cersei given that she's  normally fighting almost everyone she's  in a scene with then in the intellectual  pairing of elena and cersei is always  fun to watch ramsay bamboozles theon and  when theon spills the beans we get a  very intimate moment with him and how it  was an utter mistake by betraying his  true family funny contrast that we go  from that to immediately getting  tortured tywin talks to cersei and any  taiwan scene is basically ester  political entry gets spiked with a fun  pairing between varys and elena their  playful banter between each other shows  their sharp wits and seeing these two  veterans scheme together is legendary  sansa finally gets some hope in her life  the mutiny at crosser's keep is insane  and the irony of giara mormont dying in  the most unhonorable way is delicious  the hound roasting the brotherhood never  gets old and something big finally  happens in daenerys's storyline  throughout season 2 she's mostly had a  lackluster journey through qarth but now  that she's actually acquiring power in a  badass way it makes your plotline  infinitely more entertaining ironically  i think this is her most triumphant  moment in her overall story given her  victories are a tad bit underwhelming  and we finally get the conqueror we've  been waiting for  number 7  baylor  similar to the line in the rose this  episode is primarily here for the  execution of ned stark i do like this  episode a bit more because there are two  amazing precursor scenes one of them  being jon's talk with maester aemon in  which he's revealed he's actually a  targaryen it makes the advice he gives  jon have a lot of validity given the  amount of personal conflict eamon went  through when his family got wiped out by  robert's rebellion but he couldn't do  anything because he was swarmed to the  night's watch and we get the love is the  death of duty amen targaryen is a  literal goat and he steals so many  scenes he's in  the other scene that i thoroughly enjoy  is tyrion shay and braun bonding over  tyrion's game of guessing their past  that's actually a brilliant way to  disguise character background exposition  as natural dialogue by making it a fun  game while also adding in the fact that  turin makes assumptions on character  types the twist that shay goes against  every trait that tyrion assumes of her  makes her so much more interesting while  turing simultaneously gets rolled by her  i'd love these chill scenes where  characters go on about their past like  tyrion talking about taisha and screwed  up things taiwan has done to him some  other noteworthy moments are the debate  between importance of honor versus  survival with varys and ned showing how  slimy and weird walder frey is jon  getting longclaw and celebrating with  the boys sir jorah defeating a dothraki  bloodrider in single combat and robstark  winning his first battle the one hiccup  in this episode is the cop-out of not  showing the battle but it's definitely  understandable from a budget's  standpoint and the fact that it wasn't  entirely essential to the plots it's  just annoying that they built up to it  so much and didn't give it proper payoff  then you know there's one other small  scene left where the main character dies  nothing big or anything the  ramifications of this single event set  the entire show into a blazing path of  riveting storytelling this moment is  single-handedly one of the best twists  in cinema given how expertly it plays  against the expectations of our main  hero surviving every major obstacle we  are even led to believe for the last two  episodes that no harm will come to ned  but by going against that notion it  makes the twist even more shocking and  the execution of the scene is extremely  visceral the sound drowning out so we  only hear ned's breathing gives the  moment a haunting layer of surrealism as  we see our favorite character lose his  head  number six  you win or you die  god damn what an ending the culmination  of all the political intrigue brewing  into a twisting hurricane of knives and  backstabs in which littlefinger betrays  ned was perfect that iconic image of  littlefinger holding a knife to ned's  throat is burned into my mind as to what  game of thrones is all about we even  have more top 25 moments within this  episode given that we have the  introductory scene with tywin what a  badass man i love how it isn't even  required for him to be scanning a deer  but he just is it shows off how  resourceful he is how he isn't afraid of  getting his hands dirty and it  foreshadows him defeating the baratheons  giving his gutting their sigil the  efficiency of storytelling here is  astounding because we both learned so  much about taiwan while also learning  nuggets of wisdom tied to the lannister  psyche and this also isn't just a time  when introduction but it establishes his  relationship with jaime and how much  value he places in his children  keeping the fire hot after the start of  the episode this leads into ned  confronting cersei about her having kids  with jaime i love how she doesn't even  deny it here and instead goes into a  monologue about why she made all those  choices it allows us to actually  sympathize with a villain for once along  with having some killer dialogue  littlefinger coaches some horrors from  the sidelines and this may not seem like  an important scene but we actually  learned some motivation for  littlefinger's character theon harasses  osha but gets rolled and smoked by  maester lewin i thought she was our  prisoner are the two mutually exclusive  in your experience  robert baratheon dies rests his  beautiful and sexy soul even on his  deathbed robert has more charisma than  most of the characters in season 8. the  man is a living legend he's over here  coughing up blood while making funny  jokes give us a robert's rebellion spin  off you cowards  sergiora ejects an imposter in a tense  scene and the boys become men  ned stark attempts playing the political  game with littlefinger and renly and  it's fun re-watching this scene because  since ned is honorable by refusing to  back anyone but stannis the one true  king he ends up alienating any of his  potential allies  then finally we have kal droga being the  coolest human being in the galaxy i  adore how much jason momoa is slaying in  this role he's having the time of his  life  number five  fire and blood  to me this is the quintessential game of  thrones episode it's very well rounded  in the sense that we get excellent  scheming betrayal heartbreak and  triumphs amongst these riveting plot  lines i love the ending and how the  writers perfectly align each of the main  subplots in which they hit a similar  thematic beat of heading into the  unknown of characters starting their own  personal journeys i love seeing the  death of ned stark ripple throughout  westeros and seeing each character deal  with their own sorrow i love the vile  turn of joffrey fully embracing his  demon side and becoming a fully  blossomed villain i love the irony of  the strongest warrior dying to an  infection which shatters our  expectations i love varys and  littlefinger talking about their epic  aspirations every scene is basically  perfect even this small scene with  maester pai still talking about past  scenes is engrossing as hell and meme  worthy too if this episode had a little  bit more groundbreaking sauce in it then  it'd easily be within my top 3 but i  can't really fault the episode that much  given that ned stark died the episode  prior  number 4 kissed by fire  exploding onto the scene with a trial by  combat for the hound this episode  immediately grabs your attention and  excitement the fight is adrenaline  feeling each character is bashing  through the environment obliterating  shields and the hound cuts through  barrack's sword and the twist of berry  coming back to life elevates the scene  even higher by opening up a whole new  magical avenue of resurrections  jon snow gets to the finish line with  egret and is essentially at his happiest  ruse bolton has a maniacal cute little  moment with jaime where he delays  telling him whether or not cersei got  killed during the blackwater elena mops  the floor with tyrion in their first  dialogue scene together and it just  makes me love her to death the fact that  she shows up and dances around one of  the wittiest characters makes her a  legend easily one of the best characters  next to tywin arya has a tragic moment  with gendry where he makes the decision  to stay with the brotherhood she's come  to essentially love him and is the last  person she has left in terms of inner  family then she has a conversation with  barrick and thoros and we should learn  more about resurrections also this line  from arya is depressing  could you bring back a man without a  head not six times  just once the downfall rob stark worsens  through his trail of deteriorating  decisions and this episode has arguably  one of the best dialogue scenes in the  show in which we get a monologue from  jaime talking about how he had to kill  the mad king to save king's landing  jaime shows away his exterior armored  shell and our outlook of jaime evolves  into a more heroic night i also love how  this revelation blowers align of which  oaths you should follow and the acting  here oh  chef's kiss  this is the equivalent of tyrion's trial  scene but for jaime after that  masterpiece we then get one of the most  heartwarming scenes with davos and  shireen hanging out in the dungeon as  she teaches him how to read that  beautiful look he gives her makes my  heart flutter  greyworm establishes he respects and is  devoted to daenerys in an uplifting  scene that can promise her army isn't  just a drone of mindless killers  littlefinger is still oozing wisdom as  always and the episode ends in an  explosive family conversation between  cersei tyrion and tywin the fact that he  demolishes his own children makes him  even more of a savage tyrion and cersei  have essentially had impunity for the  last couple of seasons and are finally  put into place by this absolute sigma  chad  number three  the reigns of castamere  yeah you know why this episode is here  the red wedding is easily the best scene  in game of thrones due to how shocking  and brutal the twist was george the mad  lad martin got away with killing two  main characters  the whole aura of this scene exudes a  feeling that something feels off due to  the fact that it's so elongated there's  also a lot of signs leading up to the  betrayal that rewards viewers who really  pay attention like ruse refusing to  drink wine the phrase casually locking  the doors and the musicians playing the  reigns of castamere  god just the execution is perfect  from the acting the brute force change  in pace and the grotesque violence that  shatters our feelings and makes us  shower tears for the characters we loved  and now the question is does the rest of  the episode have the consistency they  call the overall package outstanding the  answer is a definite yes robstark making  a mental walder fury has a lot of  tension and we are curious about whether  or not he is holding a grudge which he  kind of drop that suspicion once they  commit to marrying edmure tully and then  they kill everyone any scene with the  hound in arya is pog and in this episode  there's two of them bran works into  hodar for the first time to stop him  from shouting which is cool and jon snow  finally changes sides when he's asked to  kill an innocent man the action here  feels a bit unrealistic though given  that jon has to fight a 1v3 or 4 against  the wildlings it falls into the action  trope of fighting one person at a time  to mask the fact that it's impossible to  fight a group at once and to get around  that they cut to torment wrestling egret  and also cut over to bran the direwolves  do help but that isn't until later on  and this is only a very small issue the  battle for yonkai does a much better job  at showcasing fighting a group of  enemies given that grey worm sergiora  and daario are all back to back all in  all though the reigns of cassamir is  basically filled in with good scenes  while having a perfect final sequence  number two  black water  now we're getting to the episodes that  are a complete banger through and  through the battle sequence is the main  draw here but even the dialogue scenes  leading up to the battle are amazing we  have great conversations like davos  contrasting mathos blind loyalty and  cockiness with his pragmatic view of the  difficulty of cracking king's landing  varus telling tyrion about how much he  hates magic and we have bronze funny  humor i saw you kill a man with a shield  you'll be unstoppable with an axe then  for scenes that aren't driven by  dialogue like braun and the hound  standoff they are so engaging because of  how much they raise attention in an  already stressful environment  george rr martin does a magnificent job  of stacking up conflict to make the  upcoming obstacle of sanus seem even  more daunting like everyone being scared  fighting between soldiers joffrey  abandoning everyone and people dropping  equipment that's why it's so relieving  and cathartic when tyrion explodes the  blackwater with wildfire and visually  this is the first time in the show where  you're actually awestruck the show's  ambition of massive battles is a reality  now and this explosion drastically  raises your hype for the upcoming attack  for the cinematography and action it's  all coherent and you can actually see  what's happening on screen unlike in  season 8 but for the blackwater we have  a perfect mix of wide shots to give us a  bearing of space and medium shots to  quickly cut between people fighting not  only is this battle destroy action but  there's a lot of character moments like  the hound getting traumatized by fire  and stamping at the king and tyrion's  epic speech and speaking of tyrion this  is basically his peak and i love how  happy he feels when everyone is cheering  his name after leading a successful  counter-attack one of the main reasons  why i wouldn't put this as number one is  that i think cersei's drunken dialogue  scenes grind the pace to a halt during  the battle it makes you want to skip  over them despite those scenes not  actually being bad it's cool seeing a  drunken cersei essentially letting loose  about how much she hates everything and  everyone lana heady seems like she's  having a great time in the role and  maybe they should have cut one of the  three cersei scenes but it's alright and  obviously the ending is great because it  has tywin saving the day no bias here  number one  the watchers on the wall  i think this is both one of the most  rewatchable episodes along with each and  every scene being a 9 to a 10 out of 10.  this overall episode is very similar to  blackwater but to me i like the bigger  scale of the battle the higher budget  led some more dynamic cinematography  like long takes tracking all around  castle black more grand vfx and more  ambitious action another difference is  that we have character duels like  tormund versus her alicer and jon vs  stir  it shakes up the action conflict of just  mowing down extras which can become  repetitive after a while and instead  gives us a personal 1v1 of two opposing  characters of similar skill leading to  an engaging fight and i also love how  there's so many cool moments stitched  throughout the battle like a giant  riding a mammoth dropping a scythe on  people climbing the wall a giant  shooting an arrow and impaling someone  and the boys coming together in harmony  as they chant their vows to breed  courage in the face of a charging giant  this battle is flat out perfection and  even the dialogue scenes before it don't  let up the awkward talk of john telling  sam about love is cute amon talking  about past love is pure joy that gives  us a beautiful moment of intimacy where  living in past memories can suppress the  oncoming doom and sir alistar giving jon  great advice about second guessing  yourself as a leader can be detrimental  and john actually uses that advice later  on in season seven and eight every  character has a moment to shine in this  episode and it feels perfectly balanced  jon finally gets his much deserved role  of leadership and he feels like a  complete badass once he enters the fight  sam also becomes a man and he channels  his chad energy for once by choosing to  protect the boys over hanging out with  gilly and finally the ending the death  of egret is heartbreaking and i adore  how jon is able to have a warm final  moment with her while we are swept away  by the gorgeous cinematography the  watchers on the wall easily deserves my  top spot  thank you guys for watching and for the  next ranking in the future i'll most  likely do the top 10 worst game of  thrones episodes let me know in the  comments what your top 10 would be
 

